  "response": "The most likely cause is the loyalty-discount tier in `generateInvoice`. It reads the system clock directly to decide which discount applies, so a test with a fixed `user.createdAt` gives a different discount depending on the day it runs.\n\n## What the scan points to\n\nThe scan covered the whole repo (39 findings), not just `src/billing.ts`. Only a few findings bear on a date-boundary failure in billing:\n\n| Finding | File | Relevant? |\n|---|---|---|\n| `crime_00029` direct_date, 7 clock reads (lines 57, 63, 92, 198, 238, 250, 273) | `src/billing.ts` | **Yes, this is the cause** |\n| `crime_00010` / `crime_00026` contract drift: `createdAt: number` exists only in billing's `User`/`Invoice` | `src/api/state.ts` ↔ `src/billing.ts` | Contributing |\n| `crime_00005`, `00009`, `00014` (mixed UTC/local, zoneless parse, `+86400000`) | `src/date.ts` | Real bugs, but they can't explain this |\n\nThe `src/date.ts` findings are the scan's highest-severity date problems, but they don't explain billing failures. Nothing imports `date.ts`: blast radius is 0, and `billing.ts` has no imports. That leaves `crime_00029`.\n\n## Root cause: `src/billing.ts:92`\n\n```ts\nconst daysSince = Math.floor((Date.now() - user.createdAt) / (1000 * 60 * 60 * 24));\n...\nif (daysSince > 365) discount 10% else if (> 180) 5% else if (> 90) 2%\n```\n\n**The scan underrates this one.** `crime_00029` says \"none decide a branch — all 7 record or render the reading\", so it scores low (agent_risk 0.24). That's wrong for line 92: that reading picks the discount tier, which changes `discountCents` and the total. The same pattern is copied into `recommendUpgrade` at line 198.\n\nHow this causes failures right at a boundary:\n1. **The test calendar drifts.** A fixture like `createdAt: <some fixed date>` that was \"200 days ago\" when the test was written moves into a new tier as real time passes. The test goes from passing to failing on some day with no code change.\n2. **DST and the fixed-length day.** If a test builds `createdAt` with calendar math (`d.setDate(d.getDate() - 181)`) and the range crosses a DST change, the gap in milliseconds is an hour short. `Math.floor` then gives 180, and `> 180` fails. Whether it fails depends on the host timezone and the date, which is exactly a flaky date-boundary failure.\n3. **Several clock reads per call.** Lines 57 and 92 each call `Date.now()`, so the stored `createdAt` and the discount decision can disagree.\n\n## Proposed fix\n\nRead the clock once and pass it in, with a default so existing callers keep working:\n\n```ts\nconst DAY_MS = 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000;\n\nexport function generateInvoice(\n  user: User,\n  items: InvoiceLine[],\n  paymentMethod?: string,\n  now: number = Date.now(),\n): Invoice {\n  const createdAt = now;\n  // metadata.runAt: new Date(now).toISOString(),\n  ...\n  const daysSince = Math.floor((now - user.createdAt) / DAY_MS);\n```\n\nMake the same change to `recommendUpgrade(user, now = Date.now())`. Better still, move the duplicated tier logic into one pure `loyaltyDiscountRate(daysSince)` helper. The latency timer at lines 238/250 can keep using `Date.now()`, since it only records a number.\n\nThen add tests that pin `now` and check each boundary:\n\n```ts\nconst now = Date.UTC(2026, 0, 1);\nconst user = { ...base, plan: \"pro\", createdAt: now - 181 * DAY_MS };\nexpect(generateInvoice(user, items, undefined, now).discountCents).toBe(Math.round(subtotal * 0.05));\n// likewise for exactly 180, 365 and 366 days\n```\n\nBuild test dates as `now - N * DAY_MS`, not with local `setDate`, so DST can't shift them. Also settle the `createdAt` contract drift (`crime_00010`) so every `User` has `createdAt` as epoch milliseconds.\n\n## Caveat\n\nThe current `src/billing.test.ts` doesn't call `generateInvoice` at all. It only exercises local stubs and has no real assertions (`crime_00025`, `crime_00030`), so it can't fail on this today. The diagnosis above is for the failing test you described. The boundary tests in the fix are what would actually catch this bug.",
